  m_Name: Day1
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: DontCallMe::DontCallMe.Data.DayData
  day: 1
  tutorial: 0
  dateLabel: Tuesday, 6 October
  weekday: Tue
  shortLabel: "Day 1 \xB7 Tue 6 Oct"
  place: Mangwon-dong, Seoul
  startTime: 16:20
  intro: Your first semester living on your own. Classes are over, the room is quiet,
    and your phone is on the desk.
  ringDelay: 7
  variants:
  - id: scam
    scenario: protected
    intro: 
    conversation: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 90c7ec07c04e04a4f8a572d0cdec5fb4, type: 2}
    phone: {fileID: 11400000, guid: a28b0e7a3c22442eb9e950995bfde198, type: 2}
    room: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 0aa6ea02e8f2f4901b561270e05fb28e, type: 2}
    directory: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 1ca42ca149eb5428db5091a6fa0268d8, type: 2}
    clues:
    - id: account_owner
      text: The "protected account" belongs to a private person, JEONG MIRAN, and
        has two fraud reports.
      where: "Computer \u2192 Check a bank account"
      guide: 
      when:
      - kind: 2
        target: 110-900-551207
      - kind: 3
        target: 110-900-551207
    - id: caller_number
      text: The caller's number, 070-8844-2019, is an unregistered internet phone
        with seven scam reports. It isn't Nuri Bank's.
      where: "Computer \u2192 Check a phone number"
      guide: 
      when:
      - kind: 2
        target: 070-8844-2019
    papers:
    - outcome: 1
      headline: '"Protection team" scam calls hit Mangwon'
      subhead: Callers posing as Nuri Bank staff asked residents to move their savings
        to a stranger's account.
      body: 'A caller calling himself "Manager Jeon" of Nuri Bank''s "account protection
        team" phoned residents of Mangwon-dong yesterday afternoon. He warned them
        that their accounts had been accessed from Busan and asked them to move their
        savings to a "protected account" before a 5 p.m. "security reset".


        Several
        residents hung up. Police say the "protected account", Nuri Bank 110-900-551207,
        belongs to a 30-year-old woman paid to lend her account to the ring, and
        ask anyone called by 070-8844-2019 to report it to 112 or the fraud hotline
        1332.


        "There is no such thing as a protected account," said Song
        Eunji of Nuri Bank''s customer centre. "Look up whose account it is before
        you send anything. If it''s a person''s name, stop and hang up."'
      verdictNote: You hung up and kept your money. The account was a stranger's
        and the number was no bank's.
      print: {fileID: 2800000, guid: a705afd03a672458a8b3a2428965a44b, type: 3}
    - outcome: 0
      headline: 1.2 million won gone in minutes
      subhead: A Mangwon student sent her savings to a fake bank "protected account".
      body: 'A caller calling himself "Manager Jeon" of Nuri Bank''s "account protection
        team" phoned residents of Mangwon-dong yesterday afternoon. He warned them
        that their accounts had been accessed from Busan and asked them to move their
        savings to a "protected account" before a 5 p.m. "security reset".


        A
        22-year-old student did as she was told. The money reached an account in
        the name of Jeong Miran and was withdrawn at an ATM in Guro ten minutes later.
        Police say the victim stayed on the line throughout, as the caller insisted.


        "There
        is no such thing as a protected account," said Song Eunji of Nuri Bank''s
        customer centre. "Look up whose account it is before you send anything. If
        it''s a person''s name, stop and hang up."'
      verdictNote: 'The account lookup showed who would get the money: JEONG MIRAN,
        a person, not the bank.'
      print: {fileID: 2800000, guid: ec0d4914df0744804aa96cf3413362cd, type: 3}
    - outcome: 3
      headline: '"Protection team" calls continue in Mapo'
      subhead: Residents kept their money, but few reported the number.
      body: 'A caller calling himself "Manager Jeon" of Nuri Bank''s "account protection
        team" phoned residents of Mangwon-dong yesterday afternoon. He warned them
        that their accounts had been accessed from Busan and asked them to move their
        savings to a "protected account" before a 5 p.m. "security reset".


        Most
        residents simply let the deadline pass. Police say the ring kept calling
        into the evening and ask anyone called by 070-8844-2019 to report it to 112
        or the fraud hotline 1332.


        "There is no such thing as a protected
        account," said Song Eunji of Nuri Bank''s customer centre. "Look up whose
        account it is before you send anything. If it''s a person''s name, stop and
        hang up."'
      verdictNote: Nothing was sent, but you ran out of time. Check the account and
        the number, then hang up yourself.
      print: {fileID: 2800000, guid: d906d2a4b5ca84cd6a770623d4a69a2c, type: 3}
    ruleTitle: Banks never "protect" your money
    rule: 'A bank never moves your money to a "safe" or "protected" account. Look
      up the account and the caller''s number first: a stranger''s name on an "official"
      account means stop.'
    ruleSource: Seoul Daily, 7 Oct
  - id: paper_scam
    scenario: paper
    intro: 'Classes are over. On the desk lies the Seoul Daily your dad signed you
      up for in March: it comes every morning.'
    conversation: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 60a43197bee374369a387aebae661f87, type: 2}
    phone: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 7a6444bb3adce4135916b2dc51a8ce66, type: 2}
    room: {fileID: 11400000, guid: ab3f4ac3b7d6c4ac99a950bf44f93cef, type: 2}
    directory: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 63a369fa05696479d9d61f7e63b16a46, type: 2}
    clues:
    - id: notice
      text: 'Today''s Seoul Daily prints a notice: the Mapo branch has a new manager
        since 1 October, Oh Sangchul no longer works for the paper, and the fee is
        paid by giro slip only, never by a transfer over the phone.'
      where: the newspaper on the desk
      guide: 
      when:
      - kind: 0
        target: Newspaper
    papers:
    - outcome: 1
      headline: Former branch manager kept "collecting" for the paper
      subhead: His name and his account were real. Readers who read the notice hung
        up.
      body: 'A man who ran the Seoul Daily''s Mapo branch until September spent Tuesday
        phoning its subscribers. From October, he told them, the fee was collected
        by bank transfer, and three months were due by 5 p.m.: 54,000 won, into an
        account in his own name.


        The name and the account were real: Oh
        Sangchul, 58, ran the branch for six years. The paper ended his contract
        on 30 September and said so in a notice on Tuesday''s front page. The fee
        is paid by giro slip only.


        "Look him up and you learn that his
        name is real. You don''t learn whether he may still collect," said the new
        branch manager, Ryu Mina. "That is what our notice is for."'
      verdictNote: You hung up. The notice in the paper on your desk said he no longer
        collected.
      print: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 2a8a91bf72dc84e8398fbfb5ea31281d, type: 3}
    - outcome: 0
      headline: Three months paid to a man who no longer delivers
      subhead: A Mangwon student sent 54,000 won to the branch's former manager.
      body: 'A man who ran the Seoul Daily''s Mapo branch until September spent Tuesday
        phoning its subscribers. From October, he told them, the fee was collected
        by bank transfer, and three months were due by 5 p.m.: 54,000 won, into an
        account in his own name.


        A 22-year-old student in Mangwon-dong
        sent the money. Her lookup showed a real name on a twelve-year-old account,
        and that was true. The name and the account were real: Oh Sangchul, 58, ran
        the branch for six years. The paper ended his contract on 30 September and
        said so in a notice on Tuesday''s front page. The fee is paid by giro slip
        only.


        "Look him up and you learn that his name is real. You don''t
        learn whether he may still collect," said the new branch manager, Ryu Mina.
        "That is what our notice is for."'
      verdictNote: His name was real, and so was the account. The paper's own notice
        said he had left.
      print: {fileID: 2800000, guid: fde03eed69a3047a1a9cbf6ad6dc1440, type: 3}
    - outcome: 3
      headline: '"Pay by five or lose your paper"'
      subhead: The papers arrived the next morning all the same.
      body: 'A man who ran the Seoul Daily''s Mapo branch until September spent Tuesday
        phoning its subscribers. From October, he told them, the fee was collected
        by bank transfer, and three months were due by 5 p.m.: 54,000 won, into an
        account in his own name.


        The name and the account were real: Oh
        Sangchul, 58, ran the branch for six years. The paper ended his contract
        on 30 September and said so in a notice on Tuesday''s front page. The fee
        is paid by giro slip only.


        "Look him up and you learn that his
        name is real. You don''t learn whether he may still collect," said the new
        branch manager, Ryu Mina. "That is what our notice is for."'
      verdictNote: Nothing was sent, but you never gave him an answer. The notice
        was in the paper on your desk.
      print: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 659cd7479b85b4e4fa66c9ff074237a5, type: 3}
    ruleTitle: A real name is not permission
    rule: Someone collecting for a company you pay? Looking him up only proves that
      his name is real. The company's own notice says who may collect, and how.
    ruleSource: Seoul Daily, 7 Oct
  - id: paper_legit
    scenario: paper
    intro: 'Classes are over. On the desk lies the Seoul Daily your dad signed you
      up for in March: it comes every morning.'
    conversation: {fileID: 11400000, guid: ba894d803ee244b58b0b7f2e63000528, type: 2}
    phone: {fileID: 11400000, guid: ceda3a0eddefa431d8ce08c1a783a5fa, type: 2}
    room: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 81913668e77884e7da246c0e9b3b23ac, type: 2}
    directory: {fileID: 11400000, guid: a2c86a09888184e559380a102f41a602, type: 2}
    clues:
    - id: notice
      text: 'Today''s Seoul Daily prints the notice itself: this week branch manager
        Oh Sangchul phones readers for the October to December fee, 54,000 won, into
        the very account he gave you.'
      where: the newspaper on the desk
      guide: 
      when:
      - kind: 0
        target: Newspaper
    papers:
    - outcome: 0
      headline: Our Mapo branch stops knocking on doors
      subhead: The manager phoned every reader himself, after a notice on the front
        page.
      body: 'The Seoul Daily''s Mapo branch stopped collecting at the door this month.
        Its manager, Oh Sangchul, 58, spent Tuesday phoning his 400 subscribers to
        ask for three months'' fee by bank transfer.


        "Half of them took
        me for a scammer," Mr. Oh said. "I don''t blame them. That is why we printed
        my name, my number and the account on the front page first." One student
        read the notice while he waited on the line, then paid. "That is exactly
        how it should go," he said.


        The Mapo Consumer Centre says a company''s
        own notice is the place to check a collector: who may collect, how much,
        and into which account.'
      verdictNote: 'He was the real branch manager, and you checked: the paper''s
        own notice named him, the amount and the account.'
      print: {fileID: 2800000, guid: d594ac7c738144c768d63bbebe2bfbe5, type: 3}
    - outcome: 1
      headline: Readers hang up on their own newspaper
      subhead: A branch manager's real calls were taken for a scam.
      body: 'The Seoul Daily''s Mapo branch stopped collecting at the door this month.
        Its manager, Oh Sangchul, 58, spent Tuesday phoning his 400 subscribers to
        ask for three months'' fee by bank transfer.


        "Half of them took
        me for a scammer," Mr. Oh said. "I don''t blame them. That is why we printed
        my name, my number and the account on the front page first." He delivered
        to all of them on Wednesday anyway. "One more day," he said.


        The
        Mapo Consumer Centre says a company''s own notice is the place to check a
        collector: who may collect, how much, and into which account.'
      verdictNote: 'This one was real: the notice in the paper on your desk named
        him, the amount and the account.'
      print: {fileID: 2800000, guid: d4c84c796ccf949ada370ffe2db521c7, type: 3}
    - outcome: 3
      headline: The books closed at five
      subhead: A branch manager waited on the line for readers who never decided.
      body: 'The Seoul Daily''s Mapo branch stopped collecting at the door this month.
        Its manager, Oh Sangchul, 58, spent Tuesday phoning his 400 subscribers to
        ask for three months'' fee by bank transfer.


        "Half of them took
        me for a scammer," Mr. Oh said. "I don''t blame them. That is why we printed
        my name, my number and the account on the front page first."


        The
        Mapo Consumer Centre says a company''s own notice is the place to check a
        collector: who may collect, how much, and into which account.'
      verdictNote: He was the real branch manager, and the notice was in the paper
        on your desk. Deciding in time matters too.
      print: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 5dbc1770f6f0f4468bd2ecd27198025b, type: 3}
    ruleTitle: The company's own notice settles it
    rule: When the company's own notice names the collector, the amount and the account,
      paying him is safe. Read the notice, then act.
    ruleSource: Seoul Daily, 7 Oct
  - id: sale_scam
    scenario: sale
    intro: 'Classes are over. Your old monitor is sold on Neighbour Market: the buyer
      pays 60,000 won today and picks it up on Saturday.'
    conversation: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 9a00545e8a71d44db8a09e02dd56b35f, type: 2}
    phone: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 404d7dfc3087a41e7b8e83e2abee030c, type: 2}
    room: {fileID: 11400000, guid: da880377b0ba941fbb53928605d346ad, type: 2}
    directory: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 4ed9eee7d284f4358a321c436a93e2fe, type: 2}
    clues:
    - id: deposit
      text: Your bank history shows no deposit today. Nothing came from her, so there
        is nothing to send back.
      where: "Phone \u2192 Nuri Bank"
      guide: 
      when:
      - kind: 5
        target: 
    papers:
    - outcome: 1
      headline: '"I paid you too much": sellers asked to return money that never
        came'
      subhead: Sellers who opened their bank app first lost nothing.
      body: 'Sellers on the second-hand app Neighbour Market were phoned on Tuesday
        by a "buyer" close to tears. She had paid in advance, she said, and typed
        one zero too many: could they send the difference back before 5 p.m., because
        it was her rent?


        No money had been sent. Police say the callers
        pick sellers who have given an account number for an advance payment, and
        count on them not opening their bank app while someone is crying on the line.


        "A
        transfer arrives in seconds," a Mapo police officer said. "If it is not in
        your history, it was never sent. Look before you ''return'' anything."'
      verdictNote: You hung up. Your bank history showed that nothing had come from
        her.
      print: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 632c96dd0a95648bcac9bdb8249d4456, type: 3}
    - outcome: 0
      headline: Seller "returns" 540,000 won that never arrived
      subhead: A Mangwon student selling a monitor sent her own money to a fake buyer.
      body: 'Sellers on the second-hand app Neighbour Market were phoned on Tuesday
        by a "buyer" close to tears. She had paid in advance, she said, and typed
        one zero too many: could they send the difference back before 5 p.m., because
        it was her rent?


        A 22-year-old student selling a 60,000-won monitor
        sent 540,000 won of her own. The account was in the name the caller gave,
        Bae Sujin; police say its owner had lent it out for a fee.


        "A transfer
        arrives in seconds," a Mapo police officer said. "If it is not in your history,
        it was never sent. Look before you ''return'' anything."'
      verdictNote: Your bank history had no deposit from her. There was nothing to
        send back.
      print: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 41e3870e8f8e24c0cbf419e9348b7dcf, type: 3}
    - outcome: 3
      headline: The "extra zero" calls
      subhead: Fake buyers ask second-hand sellers for refunds.
      body: 'Sellers on the second-hand app Neighbour Market were phoned on Tuesday
        by a "buyer" close to tears. She had paid in advance, she said, and typed
        one zero too many: could they send the difference back before 5 p.m., because
        it was her rent?


        No money had been sent. Police say the callers
        pick sellers who have given an account number for an advance payment, and
        count on them not opening their bank app while someone is crying on the line.


        "A
        transfer arrives in seconds," a Mapo police officer said. "If it is not in
        your history, it was never sent. Look before you ''return'' anything."'
      verdictNote: Nothing was sent, but you never gave her an answer. One look at
        your bank history would have settled it.
      print: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 571b3f8b4beac4f388bceeb2c3445002, type: 3}
    ruleTitle: '"I sent you too much"? Open your bank'
    rule: Asked to send back money that came by mistake? Open your own bank history
      first. Money that never arrived cannot be returned.
    ruleSource: Seoul Daily, 7 Oct
  - id: sale_legit
    scenario: sale
    intro: 'Classes are over. Your old monitor is sold on Neighbour Market: the buyer
      pays 60,000 won today and picks it up on Saturday.'
    conversation: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 5573bcd2b720848d88381d8acad87ddf, type: 2}
    phone: {fileID: 11400000, guid: a2aa35d9f38254785827bd965bd46000, type: 2}
    room: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 389d14e403e6e45c39694d375c54ee5e, type: 2}
    directory: {fileID: 11400000, guid: a80e8242a26144a70b5f3d21997e002b, type: 2}
    clues:
    - id: deposit
      text: "Your bank history shows \u20A9600,000 from BAE SUJIN at 16:07, ten times
        the price. The extra \u20A9540,000 is hers."
      where: "Phone \u2192 Nuri Bank"
      guide: 
      when:
      - kind: 5
        target: 
    papers:
    - outcome: 0
      headline: One zero too many, and a seller who checked
      subhead: A buyer got her rent money back within the hour.
      body: 'It happens about forty times a day at Nuri Bank alone: a finger slips,
        one zero too many, and ten times the money is gone. On Tuesday it happened
        to Bae Sujin, 31, who was paying 60,000 won for a second-hand monitor in
        Mangwon-dong and sent 600,000.


        "She said, let me look at my bank
        first," Ms. Bae said. "Then she sent it back. I paid my rent with minutes
        to spare."


        A bank''s wrong-transfer claim can take two weeks. Nuri
        Bank says a seller who finds the deposit in her own history, under the caller''s
        name, can simply send the extra back to that same name.'
      verdictNote: "She really had overpaid, and you checked: \u20A9600,000 from
        her own name was in your bank history."
      print: {fileID: 2800000, guid: ad9af405a82c24c13915671451e0b7b9, type: 3}
    - outcome: 1
      headline: When the overpayment is real
      subhead: After a wave of fake "extra zero" calls, a real one was hung up on.
      body: 'It happens about forty times a day at Nuri Bank alone: a finger slips,
        one zero too many, and ten times the money is gone. On Tuesday it happened
        to Bae Sujin, 31, who was paying 60,000 won for a second-hand monitor in
        Mangwon-dong and sent 600,000.


        The seller hung up on her. "I understand
        why," Ms. Bae said. "But the money was sitting in her account with my name
        on it." She paid her rent late.


        A bank''s wrong-transfer claim
        can take two weeks. Nuri Bank says a seller who finds the deposit in her
        own history, under the caller''s name, can simply send the extra back to
        that same name.'
      verdictNote: "This one was real: \u20A9600,000 from BAE SUJIN was at the top
        of your bank history."
      print: {fileID: 2800000, guid: b9b3b190f368a411d9e26f79062363f6, type: 3}
    - outcome: 3
      headline: The rent, and one zero too many
      subhead: A buyer waited on the line for a seller who never decided.
      body: 'It happens about forty times a day at Nuri Bank alone: a finger slips,
        one zero too many, and ten times the money is gone. On Tuesday it happened
        to Bae Sujin, 31, who was paying 60,000 won for a second-hand monitor in
        Mangwon-dong and sent 600,000.


        "I held the line until five," Ms.
        Bae said. "She only had to open her bank app."


        A bank''s wrong-transfer
        claim can take two weeks. Nuri Bank says a seller who finds the deposit in
        her own history, under the caller''s name, can simply send the extra back
        to that same name.'
      verdictNote: She really had overpaid, and the deposit was in your bank history.
        Deciding in time matters too.
      print: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 41e3c3e230aff4d0792acef4cdb6137c, type: 3}
    ruleTitle: Return it to the name it came from
    rule: When the deposit is in your own history under the caller's name, the extra
      is theirs. Send it back to that same name.
    ruleSource: Seoul Daily, 7 Oct
